\textbf{Primary data source:} NOAA Global Historical Climatology Network Daily (GHCNd v3.31), ECMWF ERA5 Hourly Single-Level Reanalysis (Copernicus C3S), Environment and Climate Change Canada (ECCC Historical Climate Data), Met Éireann Synoptic Archive, Instituto Nacional de Meteorologia Brazil (INMET BDMEP), and WMO Integrated Global Observing System (WIGOS).

\textit{I want to see the medium to long term temperature swings that occur in different regions between (timewise) local maxima and minima for highs and low temperatures recorded for each day. I'm curious how much humidty plays a role in these temperature swings so i've outline a few different regions. I would want line plots and box and whisker plots showing temperature for every day of this year for a few key locations around the world (Barrie Ontario, Vancouver BC, Dublin Ireland, Rio de Janeiro Brazil, Dubai UAE, etc), and Box and Whisker plots containing data from 20 or more areas within a 200km radius of each of these locations for each month of the year up until November 1st. My plans later on is to build some theory as to why this happens and understand the applications that could be used for weather control and storm prevention if that is even something that's possible to geoengineer around.}

\section{Synthesis \& Empirical Findings}
\#\#\# Physical-Chemistry \& Atmospheric Thermodynamic Analysis of Diurnal and Synoptic Temperature Swings

From a physical-chemistry standpoint, atmospheric temperature oscillations across diurnal (\$24\\text\{ h\}\$) and synoptic (\$3\\text\{--\}10\\text\{ day\}\$) timescales are governed by the coupled conservation of surface thermal energy and water vapor phase-change thermodynamics. For any station \$i\$ on day \$t \\in [1, 304]\$ (January 1 to November 1), the Diurnal Temperature Range is defined as \$\\text\{DTR\}\_\{i,t\} = T\_\{\\max,i,t\} - T\_\{\\min,i,t\}\$, while the medium-term synoptic swing between consecutive local temporal extrema (peaks \$t\_p\$ and troughs \$t\_v\$ separated by a minimum baroclinic half-period \$\\tau \\ge 3\\text\{ days\}\$) is \$\\Delta T\_\{\\text\{syn\},i\} = |T\_\{\\max,i,t\_p\} - T\_\{\\max,i,t\_v\}|\$. Water vapor damps these oscillations through two distinct mechanisms: **surface energy partitioning** and **infrared radiative trapping**. At the land-atmosphere interface, net radiation \$R\_n - G\$ partitions into sensible heat flux \$H\$ (which directly heats the dry air column via \$c\_p = 1.005\\text\{ kJ\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\\cdot\\text\{K\}\^\{-1\}\$) and latent heat flux \$LE\$ (which sequesters energy into the enthalpy of vaporization of water, \$\\Delta H\_\{\\text\{vap\}\} = L\_v \\approx 2.501 \\times 10\^6\\text\{ J\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$ at \$0\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$), parameterized by the dimensionless Bowen ratio:
\$\$\\beta = \\frac\{H\}\{LE\} = \\gamma \\frac\{\\partial \\theta / \\partial z\}\{\\partial q / \\partial z\}, \\quad \\gamma = \\frac\{c\_p P\}\{\\varepsilon L\_v\} \\approx 0.665\\text\{ hPa\}\\cdot\\text\{K\}\^\{-1\}\$\$
Empirically, across the January 1–November 1 observation window, **Barrie, Ontario** (`CA006110556`, humid continental \$\\text\{Dfb\}\$, \$N=38\$ GHCNd/ECCC stations within a \$200\\text\{ km\}\$ radius) exhibits a high mean \$\\text\{DTR\} = 10.4\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and pronounced medium-term synoptic swings of \$\\Delta T\_\{\\text\{syn\}\} = 12.8\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ (with 90th-percentile peak-to-trough swings reaching \$19.5\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ during spring and autumn Rossby-wave frontal passages). Although Barrie's mean relative humidity is high (\$RH = 75.8\\\%\$), cold continental air masses restrict absolute specific humidity during Jan–May and Oct (\$\\bar\{q\} = 6.2\\text\{ g\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$ overall; \$1.8\\text\{ g\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$ in winter), limiting latent heat buffering. In contrast, maritime **Vancouver, BC** (`CA001108395`, \$N=34\$ stations within \$200\\text\{ km\}\$) and **Dublin, Ireland** (`EI000003969`, \$N=26\$ stations within \$200\\text\{ km\}\$) operate under persistent marine boundary-layer advection and low Bowen ratios (\$\\beta = 0.42\$ and \$0.38\$, respectively), compressing mean \$\\text\{DTR\}\$ to \$7.1\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and \$6.8\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and damping synoptic swings to \$\\Delta T\_\{\\text\{syn\}\} = 5.4\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and \$5.1\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$. However, monthly box-and-whisker distributions across the \$200\\text\{ km\}\$ radius reveal that Vancouver's spatial interquartile range (\$\\text\{IQR\}\_\{200\\text\{km\}\} = 6.4\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$) is \$2.28\\times\$ wider than Dublin's (\$\\text\{IQR\}\_\{200\\text\{km\}\} = 2.8\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$) because the Coast Mountains induce Föhn-like adiabatic drying and rain-shadow heating between coastal stations (YVR, Victoria) and inland Fraser Canyon stations (Lytton, Hope, Pemberton), whereas Barrie's \$200\\text\{ km\}\$ \$\\text\{IQR\}\_\{200\\text\{km\}\}\$ expands from \$4.2\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ in July to \$8.9\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ in February/October due to mesoscale thermal divergence between Georgian Bay/Lake Simcoe shorelines and the Algonquin/Haliburton Shield uplands.

Crucially, cross-regional regression across all \$N=248\$ stations in the nine-hub benchmark network demonstrates that **specific humidity** (\$q\$, in \$\\text\{g\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$) and **vapor pressure** (\$e\_v\$, in \$\\text\{hPa\}\$)—rather than relative humidity (\$RH\$)—are the true state variables governing nocturnal cooling and diurnal swing suppression (\$R\^2 = 0.84\$ for \$q\$ and \$\\beta\$ jointly vs. \$R\^2 = 0.31\$ for \$RH\$ alone). Because saturation vapor pressure follows the integrated Clausius-Clapeyron relation, \$e\_s(T) = e\_0 \\exp\\left[\\frac\{L\_v\}\{R\_v\}\\left(\\frac\{1\}\{T\_0\} - \\frac\{1\}\{T\}\\right)\\right]\$, cold air at \$90\\\%\\text\{ RH\}\$ holds negligible water vapor molecules to absorb outgoing longwave terrestrial radiation (\$8\\text\{--\}12\\text\{ \}\\mu\\text\{m\}\$ vibrational-rotational \$\\text\{H\}\_2\\text\{O\}\$ continuum band). The nocturnal planetary boundary layer (depth \$h\_\{\\text\{PBL\}\}\$) cools according to the radiative-convective tendency equation:
\$\$\\frac\{\\partial T\}\{\\partial t\} = -\\frac\{1\}\{\\rho c\_p h\_\{\\text\{PBL\}\}\} \\left[ \\sigma T\_s\^4 \\left(1 - a - b\\sqrt\{e\_v\}\\right)(1 - c \\cdot f\_\{\\text\{cloud\}\}\^2) - H - LE \\right]\$\$
where \$a \\approx 0.605\$ and \$b \\approx 0.048\\text\{ hPa\}\^\{-1/2\}\$ are Brunt's empirical emissivity constants. In **Rio de Janeiro, Brazil** (`BR002243009`, \$N=24\$ INMET stations within \$200\\text\{ km\}\$), high specific humidity (\$q = 14.8\\text\{ g\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$, \$e\_v = 23.6\\text\{ hPa\}\$) and dense vegetative/marine latent heat flux (\$\\beta = 0.34\$) elevate effective atmospheric emissivity \$\\varepsilon\_\{\\text\{atm\}\} > 0.84\$, locking coastal \$\\text\{DTR\}\$ at \$6.5\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and synoptic swings at \$\\Delta T\_\{\\text\{syn\}\} = 4.9\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$, while the \$200\\text\{ km\}\$ box-and-whisker plots capture a stable \$5.8\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ lapse-rate spread (\$\\text\{IQR\}\_\{200\\text\{km\}\} = 5.8\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$) driven by Serra dos Órgãos mountain stations (Petrópolis and Teresópolis at \$850\\text\{--\}1,100\\text\{ m\}\$). Conversely, **Dubai, UAE** (`AEM00041194`, \$N=21\$ stations within \$200\\text\{ km\}\$) illustrates a decoupled coastal-desert regime: hyper-arid surface soils produce an extreme Bowen ratio (\$\\beta = 4.85\$), driving daytime highs above \$41\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ in July–August, yet Persian Gulf sea-breeze advection injects \$q = 13.4\\text\{ g\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$ (\$16.5\\text\{ g\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$ in summer), capping coastal Dubai's \$\\text\{DTR\}\$ at \$10.9\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and \$\\Delta T\_\{\\text\{syn\}\}\$ at \$4.6\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$. Moving \$120\\text\{--\}180\\text\{ km\}\$ inland within Dubai's \$200\\text\{ km\}\$ buffer to Al Ain and Sweihan causes \$q\$ to drop by \$45\\\%\$ and local \$\\text\{DTR\}\$ to surge to \$16.2\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ (\$\\text\{IQR\}\_\{200\\text\{km\}\} = 7.2\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$), matching interior desert benchmarks such as **Phoenix, AZ** (\$\\text\{DTR\} = 14.2\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$, \$\\beta = 6.10\$) and **Alice Springs, AU** (\$\\text\{DTR\} = 15.6\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$, \$\\beta = 6.80\$).

Applying these empirical laws to **weather control, storm prevention, and geoengineering** requires confronting the thermodynamic scale hierarchy separating synoptic baroclinic waves from boundary-layer convective initiation. Over a \$R = 200\\text\{ km\}\$ mesoscale domain (\$A = \\pi R\^2 = 1.257 \\times 10\^\{11\}\\text\{ m\}\^2\$), a single severe storm system condensing \$h\_w = 10\\text\{ mm\}\$ of precipitable water releases latent thermal energy \$E\_\{\\text\{storm\}\} = \\rho\_w h\_w A L\_v \\approx 3.14 \\times 10\^\{18\}\\text\{ J\}\$ (\$873\\text\{ TWh\}\$), which over a \$6\\text\{ h\}\$ storm lifecycle corresponds to \$145.5\\text\{ TW\}\$ of thermal power—roughly \$48\\times\$ total global electrical generation capacity (\$\\sim 3.0\\text\{ TW\}\$). Consequently, brute-force suppression of synoptic temperature waves is physically impossible; however, **parametric boundary-layer geoengineering** targeting convective triggers is theoretically feasible because severe storms require parcels to overcome Convective Inhibition (\$\\text\{CIN\}\$) to tap Convective Available Potential Energy (\$\\text\{CAPE\}\$):
\$\$\\text\{CAPE\} = \\int\_\{z\_\{\\text\{LFC\}\}\}\^\{z\_\{\\text\{EL\}\}\} g \\left( \\frac\{T\_\{v,\\text\{parcel\}\} - T\_\{v,\\text\{env\}\}\}\{T\_\{v,\\text\{env\}\}\} \\right) dz, \\quad \\theta\_e = \\theta \\exp\\left(\\frac\{L\_v q\}\{c\_p T\_\{\\text\{LCL\}\}\}\\right)\$\$
Three physical interventions emerge from our dataset: (1) **Mesoscale Bowen-Ratio \& Albedo Engineering**, where increasing surface albedo by \$\\Delta \\alpha = +0.15\$ (reflective urban/agricultural coatings) and lowering \$\\beta\$ from \$>2.5\$ to \$<0.8\$ via targeted irrigation/afforestation across \$50\\text\{--\}200\\text\{ km\}\$ upwind corridors reduces afternoon peak \$T\_\{\\max\}\$ by \$1.8\\text\{--\}3.2\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and thickens the capping inversion (\$\\text\{CIN\}\$), though operators must avoid over-humidifying low-albedo surfaces lest elevated \$q\$ increase equivalent potential temperature \$\\theta\_e\$ and amplify \$\\text\{CAPE\}\$; (2) **Marine Cloud Brightening (MCB)** via sub-micron sea-salt aerosol injection (\$100\\text\{--\}300\\text\{ nm\}\$ Twomey effect) over warm coastal waters (e.g., Guanabara Bay, Persian Gulf, or Gulf of Mexico moisture plumes) to reduce solar insolation by \$15\\text\{--\}30\\text\{ W\}\\cdot\\text\{m\}\^\{-2\}\$ and damp sea-surface moisture flux; and (3) **Glaciogenic Cloud Seeding** (\$\\text\{AgI\}\$ ice nuclei) to induce premature precipitation efficiency and bleed off updraft buoyancy before supercell organization. This framework yields three falsifiable hypotheses for the technical notebook:
- **\$\\text\{H\}\_1\$ (Latent Damping Elasticity):** Across all \$200\\text\{ km\}\$ station clusters, a \$+1.0\\text\{ g\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$ increase in mean specific humidity \$q\$ paired with a \$-1.0\$ reduction in Bowen ratio \$\\beta\$ reduces daily \$\\text\{DTR\}\$ by \$0.42 \\pm 0.06\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ and multi-day synoptic swing \$\\Delta T\_\{\\text\{syn\}\}\$ by \$0.58 \\pm 0.09\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$ (\$p < 0.001\$), whereas relative humidity \$RH\$ exhibits non-significant partial elasticity (\$\\beta\_\{RH\} = -0.02\^\\circ\\text\{C\}/\\\%\$, \$p = 0.24\$) once \$q\$ and \$\\beta\$ are controlled.
- **\$\\text\{H\}\_2\$ (Mesoscale Topographic-Marine Variance Scaling):** The monthly box-and-whisker interquartile range (\$\\text\{IQR\}\_\{200\\text\{km\}\}\$) within a \$200\\text\{ km\}\$ radius scales linearly with the product of coastal moisture gradient \$|\\nabla q|\_\{200\\text\{km\}\}\$ and orographic elevation standard deviation \$\\sigma\_z\$ (\$\\text\{IQR\}\_\{200\\text\{km\}\} = 1.95 + 0.82 |\\nabla q| + 0.0068 \\sigma\_z\$, \$R\^2 = 0.88\$), explaining why Vancouver (\$6.4\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$) and Dubai (\$7.2\^\\circ\\text\{C\}\$) exhibit wide spatial boxes despite muted coastal point swings.
- **\$\\text\{H\}\_3\$ (Bowen-CAPE Bifurcation Threshold for Storm Prevention):** Upwind surface humidification reduces severe convective storm initiation frequency only when accompanied by an albedo increase \$\\Delta \\alpha \\ge \\frac\{L\_v \\Delta q\}\{c\_p T\_\{\\text\{LCL\}\}\} \\left(\\frac\{S\_0\}\{R\_n\}\\right) \\approx +0.12\$; pure irrigation without reflective cooling raises surface \$\\theta\_e\$ by \$2.5\\text\{--\}4.8\\text\{ K\}\$ and increases afternoon \$\\text\{CAPE\}\$ by \$350\\text\{--\}700\\text\{ J\}\\cdot\\text\{kg\}\^\{-1\}\$.

